The engine reuses the package-level language machinery
(\cref{ch:overview}) rather than inventing its own, so the whole game shares
one default and one fallback rule.  Importing [[WELCOME]] as well lets the
engine greet the player without restating wording already defined for the
package's own greeting.

<<imports>>=
from dataclasses import dataclass

from maventyr import DEFAULT_LANGUAGE, WELCOME, localize
@

\section{Commands: what the player wants to do}

A command is a single player intention --- look around, go north, answer a
question --- captured as data, with no trace of whether it arrived as a typed
line, a button press, or a script.  We model the family as a base class with
one frozen dataclass per intention, rather than a string verb plus a bag of
arguments, because each intention carries different data and the frontends
dispatch on it: matching on [[Look]] versus a future [[Go]] and binding their
fields is clearer and safer than switching on a string and indexing a dict.

Freezing the dataclasses makes commands hashable and compared by value, and
safe to keep in a log or an undo history --- a command records what was
asked, and nothing should mutate it after the fact.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Command:
    """Base class for a player's intention, independent of any frontend."""
@

The parser in a later chapter will produce these from text; for now we need
one concrete command to exercise the engine.  [[Look]] is the natural choice:
it asks only to perceive the surroundings, so it carries no data and stays
meaningful once there is a world to describe.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Look(Command):
    """Request a description of the player's current surroundings."""
@

\section{Events: what the engine reports}

An event is something the engine reports as having happened, again as data a
frontend renders however it likes.  Keeping events inert --- text to show,
not a call that shows it --- is exactly what lets one engine drive both a
terminal and a browser: the engine decides \emph{what} to say, each frontend
decides \emph{how}.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Event:
    """Base class for something the engine reports to the frontend."""
@

The one event we need now carries a line of prose for the player.  Later
chapters add richer events --- a move, a blocked door, a posed question ---
but each is just another frozen dataclass the frontends learn to render.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Message(Event):
    """A line of text for the player to read."""

    text: str
@

\section{The engine}

Before the engine itself we need one more localized string: the reply when
the player asks for something the engine cannot yet do.  We store it as a
constant directory, like every other user-facing string, so the
not-understood reply is translated in step with the rest of the game.

<<constants>>=
DONT_UNDERSTAND = {
    "sv": "Jag förstår inte vad du menar.",
    "en": "I don't understand what you mean.",
}
@

The engine holds the one piece of state it can have at this milestone: the
language it speaks.  We keep the language on the engine rather than threading
it through every call because it is a property of the running game, not of an
individual command; later the command-line interface reads it from
configuration and passes it here, and the engine localizes every event it
emits in that one language.

<<classes>>=
class Engine:
    """Maps player commands to the events they produce.

    The engine speaks no user interface: callers pass a Command to
    ``handle`` and render the returned Events themselves.
    """

    def __init__(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
        self.language = language

    <<engine methods>>
@

\subsection{Handling a command}

[[handle]] is the whole command\,$\to$\,events contract in one method: a
command goes in, a list of events comes out.  We dispatch with a [[match]]
statement because adding a command later means adding a [[case]] --- the
structure is the extension point every future chapter reuses, and pattern
matching binds a command's fields in the same step that selects it.

The known arm is a placeholder for now.  Until there is a world to describe,
[[Look]] borrows the welcome greeting as a stand-in; once locations exist
(\cref{ch:overview} lists them among the later chapters) it will return the
current location's description instead.  We wire the arm up anyway so the
contract and its test exist before the content does.

The wildcard arm is deliberate.  A command whose type the engine does not yet
implement is not an error to crash on but a feature not built --- so the
engine replies, in the player's language, that it did not understand, and the
game plays on.  This is a different concern from the parser's job of
rejecting unparseable \emph{text}: by the time a command reaches [[handle]]
it is already a well-formed [[Command]]; the wildcard catches \emph{kinds} of
command the engine has no rule for yet.

<<engine methods>>=
def handle(self, command):
    """Return the list of events produced by ``command``."""
    match command:
        case Look():
            return [Message(localize(WELCOME, self.language))]
        case _:
            return [Message(localize(DONT_UNDERSTAND, self.language))]
@

Let's verify both arms, and that the engine honours its language.  A [[Look]]
is greeted in the engine's language --- Swedish by default, English when so
configured --- which checks the known-command path and proves the language is
a real seam rather than a hard-coded default:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
import inspect

import maventyr.game
from maventyr.game import *


def test_look_is_greeted_in_default_language():
    assert Engine().handle(Look()) == [Message(WELCOME["sv"])]


def test_look_is_greeted_in_configured_language():
    assert Engine(language="en").handle(Look()) == [Message(WELCOME["en"])]
@

An unhandled command falls through to the wildcard and yields the
not-understood reply, still localized; and whatever the command, [[handle]]
always returns a list of [[Event]]s, the shape every frontend relies on:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_unknown_command_is_not_understood():
    class Jump(Command):
        pass

    assert Engine().handle(Jump()) == [Message(DONT_UNDERSTAND["sv"])]


def test_handle_returns_a_list_of_events():
    events = Engine().handle(Look())
    assert isinstance(events, list)
    assert all(isinstance(event, Event) for event in events)
@

Finally, the rule that justifies this whole chapter: the core must touch no
user interface.  We assert it mechanically by reading the tangled module's
own source and checking that neither [[print]] nor [[input]] is called, so a
future change that smuggles I/O into the core fails the test suite rather than
a later architecture review:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_core_calls_no_print_or_input():
    source = inspect.getsource(maventyr.game)
    assert "print(" not in source
    assert "input(" not in source
@
